summary.value:
The paper presents an interesting new MIA that can improve upon prior work in a setting where attacker's knowledge is limited to white-box access to the target model and to a significant fraction of the training dataset but not training data distribution.

The initial reviews note a number of weaknesses, including
1. inability to compute attack scores incrementally and independently for new data points;
2. lack of sensitivity analysis for required coverage of training dataset and composition of the attack dataset;
3. lack of ablation studies to understand the impact of different processing steps;
4. missing reporting of computational cost;
5. results only for ResNet-18;
6. lack of comparison to state-of-the-art white-box attacks as well as label-only attacks;
7. poor explanation of mathematical symbols used.

I would also highlight two important additional weaknesses:  
8. lack of transparency in explaining the assumptions of the method in the abstract;  
9. overuse of "realistic scenario" (title + paper) and "no assumptions" (abstract + paper) while the assumptions are in fact quite restrictive, just in a different sense than some prior work.  
10. claim that existing black box attacks require knowing training hyperparameters - the work of Pradhan et al. (SaTML 2025) shows that attacks can be modified to avoid this assumption.

In particular, MIA makes most sense as an attack in a ML application using sensitive training data.  If the training data are truly sensitive (e.g. health data), it is unrealistic that an attacker would have access to a significant fraction of it. Hence calling the scenario "realistic" does not seem justified.
